// Widening: a 300-trip loop needs far more than the fixpoint's round cap to converge one step at a
// time. Widened at the loop head, `i` goes to [0, max] and the guard `i < 300` narrows it to
// [0, 299] on the edge into the body, which proves `v[i]` against the literal length 300. Without
// widening the fixpoint hit the cap and decided nothing, so the check stayed.
fn main() {
    v := u8[1; 300];
    mut i: u64 = 0;
    mut s: u64 = 0;
    while i < 300 {
        s += u64(v[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    println(s);
}
